//! # Velr Rust driver
//!
//! Velr is an embedded property-graph database queried with Cypher. This crate runs
//! the database in your application and provides parameterized queries, streaming
//! results, transactions, fulltext search, vector search, and Arrow integration.
//!
//! ## Requirements and installation
//!
//! Use Rust and Cargo on macOS (Apple Silicon or Intel), Linux (x86_64 or aarch64,
//! GNU), or Windows (x86_64, MSVC). Add the driver to `Cargo.toml`:
//!
//! ```toml
//! [dependencies]
//! velr = "0.2.62"
//! ```
//!
//! The package includes its native runtime; no server or separate runtime download
//! is required. Arrow APIs use the default `arrow-ipc` feature. Set
//! `default-features = false` if your application does not need them. Fulltext and
//! vector indexes require a file-backed database. `SEARCH` requires a Velr 0.2.62
//! or newer runtime with the corresponding search features.
//!
//! ## Quick start
//!
//! Create data with bound parameters, then read the result:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     db.run_with_params(
//!         "CREATE (:Person {name: $name, age: $age})",
//!         velr::params! { name: "Alice", age: 42_i64 }?,
//!     )?;
//!     let mut table = db.exec_one("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN p.name AS name")?;
//!     table.for_each_row(|row| {
//!         if let CellRef::Text(name) = row[0] {
//!             println!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(name)); // Alice
//!         }
//!         Ok(())
//!     })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `run` executes queries whose results you do not need. `exec_one` returns exactly
//! one result table. Handles release their resources when dropped.
//!
//! ### Optional graph analytics
//!
//! Add `velr-gds` at the same release version as the driver with
//! `cargo add velr-gds`. Enable it on each connection that needs GDS using
//! `Velr::builder().with_extension(velr_gds::Gds::new()).open(path)`. Use the
//! same `run` and `exec_one` methods for `CALL gds.*` queries. See the
//! [GDS example in the Rust guide](crate::guide#graph-data-science).
//!
//! ## Queries and results
//!
//! ### In-memory, persistent, and read-only databases
//!
//! `None` or `Some(":memory:")` opens a private in-memory database. A filesystem
//! path opens or creates a persistent database; its parent directory must exist.
//! Use `open_readonly` to read an existing database without creating, initializing,
//! migrating, or repairing it:
//!
//! ```rust,no_run
//! use velr::Velr;
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let _memory = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let file = Velr::open(Some("graph.db"))?;
//!     file.run("CREATE (:Person {name:'Alice'})")?;
//!     drop(file);
//!     let reader = Velr::open_readonly("graph.db")?;
//!     let mut count = reader.exec_one("MATCH (n) RETURN count(n) AS count")?;
//!     count.for_each_row(|row| { println!("{row:?}"); Ok(()) })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ### Database creation options
//!
//! `Velr::create(path, options)` (Velr 0.2.62+) creates a new in-memory database
//! (`None`) or a new file. It errors if the destination exists;
//! use `open` or `open_readonly` to reopen a file. `CreateOptions::new()` uses the
//! default configuration. To select an inline relationship property, pass
//! `CreateOptions::new().with_inline_edge_properties(["timestamp"])` as `options`.
//! Choose zero or one nonempty, case-sensitive name without NUL bytes; it applies to every
//! relationship type, persists on reopen, and cannot be changed afterward. Inspect
//! it with `db.inline_edge_properties()`. Read/write that property through normal
//! Cypher; selecting it does not restrict the values your application can store.
//!
//! ### Choosing an execution method
//!
//! | Task | API |
//! |---|---|
//! | Execute a write or discard results | `run`, `run_with_params`, `run_with_options` |
//! | Read exactly one table | `exec_one`, `exec_one_with_params`, `exec_one_with_options` |
//! | Read multiple tables | `exec`, `exec_with_params`, `exec_with_options`, then `next_table` |
//! | Process rows incrementally | `TableResult::for_each_row`, or `rows` and `RowIter::next` |
//! | Collect application values | `TableResult::collect` |
//!
//! `exec_one` errors when execution produces zero or multiple result tables.
//! Transactions expose the same execution choices. Use unique projected column
//! aliases; conflicting names are query errors.
//!
//! ### Parameters and owned values
//!
//! Use `$name` in query text and omit `$` from parameter keys. Supported values are
//! nulls, booleans, signed 64-bit integers, finite floats, strings, lists, and maps.
//! Construct them with `params!`, `QueryParams`, or `QueryValue`. Strings are data,
//! never query text. Invalid parameter construction returns `QueryParamError`,
//! which converts to `velr::Error` with `?`.
//!
//! `CellRef` exposes null, bool, integer, float, text, and JSON cells. Lists, maps,
//! and graph entities use JSON bytes. Text and JSON are borrowed for the row
//! callback; copy or decode them there when retaining values:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let mut table = db.exec_one_with_params(
//!         "UNWIND $names AS name RETURN name ORDER BY name",
//!         velr::params! { names: vec!["Grace", "Ada"] }?,
//!     )?;
//!     let names: Vec<String> = table.collect(|row| match row[0] {
//!         CellRef::Text(bytes) => Ok(String::from_utf8_lossy(bytes).into_owned()),
//!         _ => Ok(String::new()),
//!     })?;
//!     println!("{names:?}"); // ["Ada", "Grace"]
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! `collect` retains every mapped value in memory. Use `for_each_row` for large
//! results. A `TableResult<'_>` borrows its connection or producing transaction
//! until the table is dropped, including after all rows have been read. Drop the
//! table before closing the connection or moving it to another thread. Drop
//! transaction streams, tables, and row cursors before committing, rolling back,
//! or dropping their transaction; Rust enforces these lifetime restrictions.
//!
//! ### Multiple result tables
//!
//! A script can return more than one table. Consume each table before requesting
//! the next:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use velr::Velr;
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let mut stream = db.exec("RETURN 'first' AS value; RETURN 'second' AS value")?;
//!     while let Some(mut table) = stream.next_table()? {
//!         println!("{:?}", table.column_names());
//!         table.for_each_row(|row| { println!("{row:?}"); Ok(()) })?;
//!     }
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! ### Row caps, deadlines, and cancellation
//!
//! `QueryOptions::max_result_rows(n)` caps emitted rows independently per table;
//! zero keeps column metadata without rows. Existing query `LIMIT` clauses apply.
//! A cap does not prevent writes or limit execution time. Combine a cap with
//! parameters through `with_params` or `with_param`.
//!
//! Use `with_timeout` for a deadline covering execution and subsequent fetching.
//! Zero duration expires immediately. Attach a `CancellationToken` when a request
//! or UI handler must be able to stop work:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use std::time::Duration;
//! use velr::{CancellationToken, ErrorKind, QueryOptions, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let stop = CancellationToken::new();
//!     let options = QueryOptions::max_result_rows(20)
//!         .with_timeout(Duration::from_secs(2))
//!         .with_cancellation(stop.clone());
//!     // Give a clone of stop to the handler that calls cancel().
//!     let result = db.exec_one_with_options("UNWIND range(1, 100) AS n RETURN n", options)
//!         .and_then(|mut table| table.for_each_row(|row| { println!("{row:?}"); Ok(()) }));
//!     if let Err(error) = result {
//!         match error.kind() {
//!             ErrorKind::Cancelled | ErrorKind::DeadlineExceeded => println!("{}", error.message),
//!             _ => return Err(error),
//!         }
//!     }
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A token can be canceled from another thread and stays canceled after signaling.
//! Create a fresh token for independent operations. Check execution and fetch errors.
//!
//! ## Transactions
//!
//! ### Commit, rollback, and savepoints
//!
//! Use a transaction for changes that must succeed together. `commit()` persists
//! its work; `rollback()` or dropping an active transaction discards it:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use velr::Velr;
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let tx = db.begin_tx()?;
//!     tx.run("CREATE (:Person {name:'Alice'})")?;
//!     {
//!         let savepoint = tx.savepoint()?;
//!         tx.run("CREATE (:Person {name:'Temporary'})")?;
//!         savepoint.rollback()?;
//!     }
//!     tx.commit()?; // Keeps Alice, discards Temporary.
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A scoped savepoint's `release()` keeps its changes pending in the transaction;
//! `rollback()` or dropping it rolls back and releases it. Finish scoped savepoints
//! before finalizing the transaction.
//!
//! ### Named savepoints
//!
//! Named savepoints belong to the transaction. `rollback_to` discards newer named
//! savepoints and keeps its target available; `release_savepoint` releases the
//! newest active named savepoint. Names must be unique while active:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use velr::Velr;
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let tx = db.begin_tx()?;
//!     tx.savepoint_named("before_import")?;
//!     tx.run("CREATE (:Person {name:'Temporary'})")?;
//!     tx.rollback_to("before_import")?;
//!     tx.run("CREATE (:Person {name:'Grace'})")?;
//!     tx.release_savepoint("before_import")?;
//!     tx.commit()?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! A writable transaction reserves the writer slot when started. For a read-only
//! snapshot while another connection writes, use `open_readonly` and `begin_tx`.
//!
//! ## Errors and concurrency
//!
//! Most operations return `velr::Result<T>`. `Error` exposes a numeric operation
//! `code`, a diagnostic `message`, and `kind()` for programmatic classification.
//! Use `is_query_error()` for the Cypher error family and `detail()` to match
//! specific reasons through `ErrorDetail`, such as `DeleteConnectedNode`.
//! `Error::application(source)` converts application failures without choosing a
//! native status and preserves their original source. See the
//! [error-handling guide](crate::guide#results-lifetimes-and-threads) for examples.
//! Propagate errors from execution, `next_table`, and row consumption. A callback's
//! returned error stops iteration and is passed back unchanged.
//!
//! `Velr` is `Send` and not `Sync`: move an idle connection between threads, or use
//! one connection per thread. Transactions, streams, tables, row cursors, savepoints,
//! and explain traces are neither `Send` nor `Sync`; keep them on their creating
//! thread. An `Arc<Velr>` does not permit concurrent use of one connection.
//!
//! ## Features and integrations
//!
//! ### Quantified path patterns
//!
//! Use `{2}` after a parenthesized pattern to match exactly two repetitions.
//! Variables declared inside the pattern are lists in traversal order. The
//! example projects stop names and reads both lists as JSON cells.
//!
//! ```rust,no_run
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     db.run(
//!         "CREATE (:Stop {name: 'Central'})-[:NEXT]->(:Stop {name: 'North'})
//!          -[:NEXT]->(:Stop {name: 'Airport'})",
//!     )?;
//!     let mut route = db.exec_one(
//!         "MATCH (:Stop {name: 'Central'}) ((departure)-[:NEXT]->(arrival)){2}
//!          RETURN [stop IN departure | stop.name] AS departures,
//!                 [stop IN arrival | stop.name] AS arrivals",
//!     )?;
//!     route.for_each_row(|row| {
//!         if let [CellRef::Json(departures), CellRef::Json(arrivals)] = row {
//!             println!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(departures)); // ["Central","North"]
//!             println!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(arrivals)); // ["North","Airport"]
//!         }
//!         Ok(())
//!     })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! See the [query language reference](crate::query_language#quantified-path-patterns)
//! for repetition ranges and predicates.
//!
//! ### SEARCH clause
//!
//! In Velr 0.2.62+, use `SEARCH` with `MATCH` or `OPTIONAL MATCH` to query an existing
//! fulltext or vector index. Bind the node/relationship in the pattern, supply
//! `FOR` and a required candidate `LIMIT` inside the search parentheses, and use
//! `SCORE AS name` when you need scores. Order results explicitly with
//! `ORDER BY name DESC`. The examples below create their indexes and bind inputs.
//! The [search guide](crate::guide#search-clause) covers filters, pagination, analyzers,
//! relationship patterns, and correlated subqueries. Existing search `CALL`
//! procedures remain supported; embedder-backed text queries use vector procedures.
//!
//! ### Fulltext search
//!
//! Use a file-backed database. Create an index over text properties, then pass the
//! search string as a bound parameter:
//!
//! ```rust,no_run
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(Some("search.db"))?;
//!     db.run("MERGE (p:Paper {id:'intro'}) SET p.title='Graph databases', p.body='Store connected data'")?;
//!     db.run("CREATE FULLTEXT INDEX paperText IF NOT EXISTS FOR (p:Paper) ON EACH [p.title, p.body]")?;
//!     let mut hits = db.exec_one_with_params(
//!         "MATCH (paper) SEARCH paper IN (FULLTEXT INDEX paperText FOR $search LIMIT $limit) SCORE AS score RETURN paper.title AS title, score ORDER BY score DESC",
//!         velr::params! { search: "graph", limit: 10_i64 }?,
//!     )?;
//!     hits.for_each_row(|row| {
//!         if let CellRef::Text(title) = row[0] {
//!             println!("{}: {:?}", String::from_utf8_lossy(title), row[1]);
//!         }
//!         Ok(())
//!     })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Search accepts terms, phrases, field scoping, boolean grouping, prefixes, and
//! boosts. Higher scores rank higher within one query; scores are not normalized.
//! Writes keep indexes up to date. See the [fulltext grammar](crate::query_language#fulltext-search).
//!
//! ### Vector search and embeddings
//!
//! Store application-computed embeddings as numeric vector/list properties:
//!
//! ```rust,no_run
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(Some("search.db"))?;
//!     db.run("MERGE (c:Chunk {id:'intro'}) SET c.text='Connected data', c.embedding=[1.0,0.0,0.0]")?;
//!     db.run("CREATE VECTOR INDEX chunkEmbedding IF NOT EXISTS FOR (c:Chunk) ON (c.embedding) OPTIONS {indexConfig:{dimensions:3, metric:'cosine'}}")?;
//!     let mut hits = db.exec_one_with_params(
//!         "MATCH (chunk) SEARCH chunk IN (VECTOR INDEX chunkEmbedding FOR $vector LIMIT $limit) SCORE AS score RETURN chunk.text AS text, score ORDER BY score DESC",
//!         velr::params! { vector: vec![1.0_f64, 0.1, 0.0], limit: 5_i64 }?,
//!     )?;
//!     hits.for_each_row(|row| {
//!         if let CellRef::Text(text) = row[0] {
//!             println!("{}: {:?}", String::from_utf8_lossy(text), row[1]);
//!         }
//!         Ok(())
//!     })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Every stored/query vector must match the index dimensions; a cosine query vector
//! must contain a nonzero component. Index scores depend on the metric, with higher
//! scores ranking higher. Vector `SEARCH`, vector `CALL` procedures, and the exact
//! scalar `vector.similarity.cosine(left, right)` use the same `0..1` cosine scale.
//! For Euclidean indexes, `SEARCH` returns `1 / (1 + squaredDistance)` and the
//! procedures return `-squaredDistance`. Convert an older raw cosine procedure
//! threshold `t` to `(1 + t) / 2`.
//!
//! For text-to-vector indexes, register a connection-owned callback with
//! `register_vector_embedder`, then name it in `CREATE VECTOR INDEX ... ON EACH [...]`.
//! Return one finite `Vec<f32>` per input, with the requested dimensions and original
//! input order. Register the callback on connections that write or query that index.
//! The [embedding guide](crate::guide#vector-search) covers callback registration and options.
//!
//! ### Bulk import from CSV
//!
//! Use the standalone `velr-import` crate to create a Velr database from local
//! node and relationship CSV files. Add it alongside the query driver.
//!
//! ```sh
//! cargo add velr-import
//! ```
//!
//! Save the node data below as `people.csv`.
//!
//! ```csv
//! personId:ID(people),name:string,:LABEL
//! 1,Ada,Person
//! ```
//!
//! The driver can open the resulting file after the import returns.
//!
//! ```rust,ignore
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//! use velr_import::{import_csv, CsvImport};
//!
//! fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
//!     let report = import_csv("people.db", CsvImport::files(["people.csv"]))?;
//!     assert_eq!(report.nodes, 1);
//!
//!     let db = Velr::open_readonly("people.db")?;
//!     let mut people = db.exec_one("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN p.name AS name")?;
//!     people.for_each_row(|row| {
//!         if let CellRef::Text(name) = row[0] {
//!             println!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(name)); // Ada
//!         }
//!         Ok(())
//!     })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Use a new destination and an importer built for the same Velr release.
//! `CsvImport::files` accepts plain or compressed CSV with embedded headers.
//! Use `CsvImport::manifest("import.toml")` to group file parts or supply separate
//! headers. The `velr-import` README covers header syntax, options, and reports.
//!
//! ### Arrow import and export
//!
//! The default `arrow-ipc` feature provides IPC file/Feather v2 import and export;
//! IPC streams are not accepted. This round trip needs no extra Rust dependency:
//!
//! ```rust
//! # #[cfg(feature = "arrow-ipc")]
//! # mod arrow_example {
//! use velr::{CellRef, Velr};
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     let ipc = {
//!         let mut table = db.exec_one("UNWIND ['Ada','Grace'] AS name RETURN name")?;
//!         table.to_arrow_ipc_file()?
//!     };
//!     db.bind_arrow_ipc("people", &ipc)?;
//!     let mut table = db.exec_one("UNWIND BIND('people') AS row RETURN row.name AS name")?;
//!     table.for_each_row(|row| {
//!         if let CellRef::Text(name) = row[0] {
//!             println!("{}", String::from_utf8_lossy(name));
//!         }
//!         Ok(())
//!     })?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! # }
//! ```
//!
//! The IPC bytes are owned and remain usable after their table closes. Bind calls
//! borrow bytes only during the call. For native arrays, add `arrow2 = "0.18"` and
//! use `bind_arrow` or `bind_arrow_chunks`; see the [array example](crate::guide#arrow-ipc).
//! Export infers column types: structured values become JSON strings in Arrow UTF-8
//! columns; numeric types may widen and incompatible mixed kinds become strings.
//! Nulls remain null; empty/all-null columns use UTF-8. Inspect the exported schema.
//! Export/collection can use memory proportional to result size.
//!
//! ### Graph introspection and explain
//!
//! `SHOW INDEXES` lists Cypher-defined indexes with `name`, `state`, `type`,
//! `entityType`, `labelsOrTypes`, and `properties`. Filter/project with `YIELD`,
//! `WHERE`, and `RETURN`, for example:
//!
//! ```cypher
//! SHOW INDEXES YIELD name, type, properties
//! WHERE type = 'VECTOR' RETURN name, properties ORDER BY name
//! ```
//!
//! `CALL db.labels() YIELD label`, `CALL db.relationshipTypes() YIELD relationshipType`,
//! and `CALL db.propertyKeys() YIELD propertyKey` list graph metadata. Names also
//! include declarations on current indexes; property keys include registered unused
//! keys. `SHOW CURRENT GRAPH SHAPE` reports observed labels, relationship types,
//! properties, value types, and counts. Explain a query without executing it:
//!
//! ```rust
//! use velr::Velr;
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(None)?;
//!     db.run("CREATE (:Person {name:'Alice'})")?;
//!     let mut shape = db.exec_one("SHOW CURRENT GRAPH SHAPE")?;
//!     println!("{:?}", shape.column_names());
//!     shape.for_each_row(|row| { println!("{row:?}"); Ok(()) })?;
//!     let trace = db.explain("MATCH (p:Person) RETURN p.name AS name")?;
//!     println!("{}", trace.to_compact_string()?);
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Use `explain_with_params` for a parameterized query. `explain_analyze` executes the
//! query, including writes; use `VelrTx::explain_analyze` and roll back when those
//! writes should be discarded. `snapshot()` returns owned explain data. Diagnostic
//! text can change between versions. Use a current-format database for graph queries.
//!
//! ## Compatibility and maintenance
//!
//! Opening a database never migrates it. Inspect compatibility and explicitly
//! migrate supported older formats before graph queries, including reads:
//!
//! ```rust,no_run
//! use velr::Velr;
//!
//! fn main() -> velr::Result<()> {
//!     let db = Velr::open(Some("graph.db"))?;
//!     println!("{} -> {}", db.schema_version()?, db.current_schema_version()?);
//!     if db.needs_migration()? {
//!         let report = db.migrate()?;
//!         println!("{:?}", report.status);
//!     }
//!     db.vacuum()?;
//!     Ok(())
//! }
//! ```
//!
//! Migration and `vacuum()` require a writable connection outside an active
//! transaction; vacuum also requires the current schema. Equivalent commands are
//! `MIGRATE DATABASE` and `VACUUM DATABASE`. Databases from releases before 0.2.14
//! must be recreated by re-importing source data. The 0.2.62 runtime supports
//! migration from legacy schema versions 3 through 9. Reopen read-only connections
//! after migrating through a writable connection.
//!
//! For restricted writable directories, set `VELR_CACHE_DIR` to a writable runtime
//! cache location before the first connection. The cache filesystem must support
//! file locks. Concurrent first connections share one cached runtime copy.
//! `VELR_RUNTIME_PATH` selects a compatible runtime explicitly; normally the
//! bundled runtime is used.
